 ],
 "literal": "Red battlefield to fall, having killed, the demons who destroyed, / with straight-shafted arrows, with red-tusked elephants, / wearing warrior-anklets and armlets, Murugan's hill, / has clusters of flame lilies like blood-colored flowers.",
 "translation": "The hill of Lord Murugan, who wears warrior-anklets and armlets, who destroyed the demons, killing them so they fell on the red battlefield, with his straight-shafted arrows and red-tusked elephants, is filled with clusters of flame lilies that look like flowers of blood.",
 "modern": "செக்கச்சிவந்த போர்க்களத்தில் வீழுமாறு அசுரர்களைக் கொன்று அழித்த, செம்மையான தண்டையுடைய அம்புகளையும் செங்கோட்டு யானையையும் உடைய, வீரக்கழலும் தொடியும் அணிந்த முருகப் பெருமானுடைய மலையானது, குருதி போன்ற நிறமுடைய பூக்களைக் கொண்ட காந்தள் மலர்க் கொத்துக்களை உடையதாகும்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Kurinji (mountainous) landscape. The companion (தோழி) speaks of the hill belonging to Lord Murugan (Cēey), who is the patron deity of the Kurinji region. She describes his great battle where he destroyed the demons, turning the battlefield red with blood. The hill is filled with clusters of red flame lilies (kāntal) that resemble blood-red flowers, mirroring the imagery of the battlefield. This vivid red imagery connects the divine protector of the land with the natural beauty of the mountain, emphasizing the strength and passion associated with the region. The key words are 'சேஎய்' (Murugan), 'குன்றம்' (hill), and 'காந்தள்' (flame lily).",
